messages:
tasha: FYI customer BlueHarbor reported sudden 429s starting ~08:12 UTC. They saw repeated failures for ~5 minutes, stack traces point to rate limit errors. Asking for troubleshooting help.
dan: Can you paste the exact 429 payload + headers they receive? Need x-rate-limit values to confirm RPM vs TPM.
tasha: Pasting sample response from their app:
```
HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
x-rate-limit-limit: 600
x-rate-limit-remaining: 0
x-rate-limit-reset: 18
content-type: application/json
{"error":"rate_limit_exceeded","message":"Token bucket exhausted","code":429}
```
maria: 600 limit -> they're hitting RPM cap. Do we have tenant metadata? Are they on a burst-enabled plan?
tasha: They're on hosted standard, no burst flag. Incident timeline: they deployed order-sync v2 at 08:11 which parallelizes across 40 workers. No client-side jitter.
alex: Sounds like a classic minute-boundary stampede — parallel workers + token refresh aligning to same second. Any evidence in request timestamps?
tasha: Logs show tight alignment, sample tail below:
```
08:12:00 GET /v1/generate tenant=bh-123 reqs=38
08:12:01 GET /v1/generate tenant=bh-123 reqs=40
08:12:59 GET /v1/generate tenant=bh-123 reqs=42
```
infra-bot: ALERT: ratecap-hit tenant=bh-123 rpm=652 tpm=9120 at 2024-09-07T08:12:03Z
maria: With >600 RPM sustained they'll exhaust the bucket quickly. Root cause hypotheses: (1) client parallelism too high, (2) requests bunch at minute boundary (token refill alignment), (3) aggressive retry w/o jitter.
dan: Also check their retry policy. If they retry immediately on 429, that amplifies the problem.
tasha: They confirmed retries are immediate with up to 5 attempts, no backoff. Also token refresh is every 60s, triggered by a scheduler — aligns with start of minute.
alex: Immediate remediation options: (A) temp quota bump, (B) ask customer to cap concurrency + implement exponential backoff + jitter, (C) advise rolling restart with randomized refresh offset, (D) enable server-side smoothing for their tenant if available.
maria: I can approve a one-time temporary bump (2x RPM) for a short window while they roll back or patch. Need owner to request window length.
tasha: Customer asks for 6-hour bump while they roll back the parallel change. I'll coordinate.
dan: Also provide them a short code snippet for safe retry patterns.
tasha: Posting JS example to share with customer: 
```js
async function callWithBackoff(fn, attempts=5){
  for(let i=0;i<attempts;i++){
    try{
      return await fn();
    }catch(e){
      if(e.status !== 429) throw e;
      const backoff = Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2,i), 10000);
      const jitter = Math.floor(Math.random()*200);
      await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r, backoff + jitter));
    }
  }
  throw new Error('exhausted retries');
}
```
alex: I'll action the quota bump now: setting rpm=1200 for tenant bh-123, duration=6h. Documenting change in the console.
infra-bot: ACTION: quota-update tenant=bh-123 rpm=1200 duration=6h by=alex at 2024-09-07T08:16:30Z
blueharbor-support: Thanks — we've rolled back the parallelizer and added jitter + backoff. Errors dropped to normal levels within a minute. Appreciate the quick bump. :tada:
maria: Follow-ups: recommend they (1) cap worker concurrency to 8-12, (2) add random offset to their token refresh, (3) adopt exponential backoff w/ full jitter, and (4) open ticket if they need long-term quota increase.
dan: Also add a short runbook note: "minute-boundary fanout" pattern -> check request timestamps for alignment, look at x-rate-limit headers, check client retry behavior. I'll paste into support KB.
tasha: Closing loop with customer; I'll leave thread open for 24h to confirm no recurrence. Thanks all.
